{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Final Project and Professional Vision","title":"Portfolio Artifact Selection","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting portfolio artifact selection to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"06","section":"03","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_final_project_and_professional_vision","cat531_supplement_portfolio_artifact_selection","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/06/02"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_6_3_portfolio_artifact_selection_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_6_3_portfolio_artifact_selection_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_6_3_portfolio_artifact_selection_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_6_3_portfolio_artifact_selection_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":3,"top_tier_hardened":false},"content":"# Portfolio Artifact Selection\n\n![Portfolio Artifact Selection textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/06-03-portfolio-artifact-selection.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA teaching portfolio is an argument, not a scrapbook. Every artifact you include is a piece of evidence offered in support of a specific claim about your competence, and a reviewer reads the portfolio the way a juror reads exhibits: asking what each one actually proves. The most common portfolio failure is selecting by pride (the assignment you got an A on, the lesson that felt good) instead of by warrant (the artifact that demonstrates the exact competency the claim names). A high grade proves you satisfied an instructor; it does not, by itself, prove you can use technology to support equitable learning.\n\nSelection is therefore an evidentiary discipline. For each competency claim, you choose the smallest set of artifacts that, taken together, would convince a skeptic, and you tag each artifact with the reason it earns its place. The Design Tension Studio map is one such artifact, and choosing whether and how to include it is itself a selection decision you must be able to defend.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA candidate must evidence the claim \"uses technology to support equitable learning.\" Her instinct is to lead with her highest-scoring artifact, a polished biology slide deck. But the deck proves content mastery and design taste; it says nothing about equity of access. Meanwhile, sitting unused in her files is a one-page reflection in which she rejected a popular app because it excluded students without home devices. That reflection looks like a record of something she did not do, yet it is the single strongest piece of evidence for the equity claim, because it shows the judgment the claim names being exercised under real constraint.\n\nThe lesson is that evidentiary value and personal pride often point in opposite directions. The artifact you are proudest of may prove the wrong thing, and the artifact that looks like a near-miss may be the clearest demonstration of the competency.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nPortfolio artifact selection is the practice of matching evidence to claims by warrant, coverage, and economy:\n\n- **Warrant** is the link between an artifact and the claim it supports. An artifact has warrant for a claim only if it shows the specific competency being asserted. A grade is warrant for \"meets instructor expectations,\" not for \"supports equitable learning.\"\n- **Coverage** asks whether the chosen artifacts, together, address the full claim. A claim about equitable technology use is not covered by access alone; it may also need evidence of differentiation and of refusing a tool that excluded learners.\n- **Economy** is the discipline of choosing the fewest artifacts that establish the claim. Adding a fourth artifact that proves nothing new dilutes the argument and signals you cannot tell strong evidence from filler.\n\nA useful move is to write each artifact a one-line rationale tag stating exactly what it proves:\n\n| Artifact | Claim it warrants | Why it earns inclusion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| UDL lesson plan with access plan | Equitable design | Names concrete access supports, not just intent |\n| Screenshots of differentiated AI-feedback settings | Differentiation in practice | Shows the tool tuned for varied needs |\n| Reflection rejecting a device-dependent tool | Equity judgment | Demonstrates the value exercised against convenience |\n\nIf you cannot write a rationale tag that names a distinct contribution, the artifact does not belong in this argument. The course anchors sharpen the tags: TPACK tests whether an artifact actually shows technology serving a learning target, critical edtech studies tests whether it shows attention to who is excluded, and datafication tests whether it shows responsible handling of student data.\n\n<inline-check question=\"Your claim is 'uses technology to support equitable learning.' You want to lead with your A-graded biology slide deck. Why does it fail the warrant check for this claim?\" explanation=\"A grade is warrant for 'meets instructor expectations,' not for 'supports equitable learning.' An artifact has warrant for a claim only if it shows the specific competency being asserted; the deck proves content mastery and design taste, an adjacent competency, not equity of access.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It is not polished enough to include\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It proves an adjacent competency (content mastery), not the equity claim asserted\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It uses too much technology\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It received only an A rather than an A-plus\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nPut the three selection tests in the order you should apply them when judging a candidate artifact:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Warrant: does this artifact show the exact competency the claim names?\",\"Coverage: does the set, taken together, address the whole claim?\",\"Economy: would removing this artifact weaken the argument, or is it filler?\",\"Polish: is the kept, load-bearing artifact presentable?\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nStart with a thin claim that many students write on a first pass:\n\n> I made the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision clearer.\n\nThat claim is too thin because \"clearer\" does not identify the audience, the problem, or the evidence. A stronger claim would read:\n\n> I revised the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision so that the intended audience can see the decision, the constraint, and the evidence source. The revision is justified by **critical edtech studies**, and I can point to the exact part of the artifact that changed.\n\nThe stronger version treats the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision as evidence. It names what changed, links the change to **critical edtech studies**, and leaves a reader able to audit the decision.\n\n### Worked Example: An Annotated Selection Decision\n\nFollow a candidate selecting 3 artifacts to evidence the claim \"uses technology to support equitable learning.\"\n\n1. **The claim to evidence.** \"Uses technology to support equitable learning.\" Every selected artifact must defensibly support this exact claim.\n2. **Candidate pool (before).** Five files: (a) an A-graded slide deck on cell biology, (b) a UDL lesson plan with an access plan, (c) screenshots of differentiated AI-feedback settings, (d) a reflection rejecting a tool that excluded device-less students, (e) a recent quiz with a high score.\n3. **First instinct, then annotation.** First pick was (a) and (e) because of their grades. Reviewer note: \"These show content mastery, not equity-of-access judgment.\" The grades are not the claim.\n4. **Selection (after).** Keep (b), (c), (d). Each carries a rationale tag: (b) names the UDL access plan; (c) shows feedback tuned for varied needs; (d) shows a tool rejected on equity grounds.\n5. **Defend a kept failure.** Artifact (d) documents a rejected tool, which looks like failure but evidences reflective equity judgment, so it stays.\n\nThe before selects by grade; the after selects by what each artifact proves about the claim, and tags the reason so a reviewer can audit it.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Choosing the keystone artifact for an equity claim\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"You must evidence the claim uses technology to support equitable learning. Your pool: (a) an A-graded biology slide deck, (b) a UDL lesson plan with an access plan, (c) screenshots of differentiated AI-feedback settings, (d) a one-page reflection where you rejected a popular app because it excluded students without home devices, (e) a high-scoring quiz. Which artifact do you lead with?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"(a) the A-graded slide deck, my proudest work\",\"next\":\"pride\",\"feedback\":\"You selected by pride, not warrant. The deck proves content mastery, an adjacent competency, not equity of access.\"},{\"label\":\"(d) the rejected-app reflection\",\"next\":\"reflection\",\"feedback\":\"Counterintuitive but right: the artifact that looks like a near-miss is the clearest demonstration of the equity judgment the claim names.\"},{\"label\":\"Include all five so the portfolio looks abundant\",\"next\":\"hoard\",\"feedback\":\"Abundance without economy reads as an inability to judge evidence, undercutting the very competency a portfolio demonstrates.\"}]},\"pride\":{\"prompt\":\"The reviewer notes: this shows design taste, not equity-of-access judgment. Where is the claim actually demonstrated in your pool?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"In (d), where I rejected a tool on equity grounds\",\"next\":\"reflection\",\"feedback\":\"Yes. Evidentiary value and personal pride often point in opposite directions.\"}]},\"hoard\":{\"prompt\":\"The reviewer cannot tell which artifacts are load-bearing. Apply economy: which can be cut without weakening the case?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Cut (a) and (e); their grades prove satisfaction, not the equity competency\",\"next\":\"reflection\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. Removing them does not weaken the equity argument, so they are filler here.\"}]},\"reflection\":{\"prompt\":\"You lead with (d). A colleague objects: a reflection documenting a tool you did NOT use looks like a record of failure. How do you defend keeping it?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"It evidences reflective equity judgment: the value winning against convenience, which is exactly the claim\",\"next\":\"defend\",\"feedback\":\"Strong defense. A documented refusal can be the most persuasive evidence of professional judgment.\"},{\"label\":\"Drop it; failures do not belong in a portfolio\",\"next\":\"drop\",\"feedback\":\"This discards your single strongest piece of warrant for the claim. The refusal is the competency in action.\"}]},\"drop\":{\"prompt\":\"Without (d), what evidence remains that you exercise equity judgment under real constraint?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"None that shows the judgment in action; restore (d)\",\"next\":\"defend\",\"feedback\":\"Right, the refusal is irreplaceable warrant for this specific claim.\"}]},\"defend\":{\"prompt\":\"You keep (b), (c), and (d), each with a rationale tag. What does coverage require you to confirm before submitting?\",\"feedback\":\"Coverage asks whether the three together address the WHOLE claim: (b) names concrete access supports, (c) shows the tool tuned for varied needs, and (d) shows the equity value exercised against convenience. Together they cover design, differentiation, and judgment. A fourth artifact proving nothing new would dilute the argument, so economy says stop here. Each artifact carries a tag a skeptic would accept, which is what makes the selection auditable.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nSelect for warrant before you select for polish. Ask first \"what claim am I making,\" then \"which artifact most directly proves it to a skeptic,\" and only then \"is the artifact presentable.\" A beautiful artifact that proves the wrong claim is a weaker exhibit than a plain one that proves the right claim.\n\nRun each candidate artifact through three questions:\n\n1. **Warrant check:** Does this artifact show the exact competency the claim names, not an adjacent one?\n2. **Coverage check:** Does the set, taken together, address the whole claim rather than one slice of it?\n3. **Economy check:** Would removing this artifact weaken the argument? If not, cut it.\n\nIf an artifact fails the warrant check, no amount of polish will save its place in the portfolio.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"A peer plans to include four strong artifacts for a single claim because 'more evidence is more convincing.' Explain why economy can make the argument stronger by cutting one, and name the test you would apply to decide which to cut.\" answer=\"More artifacts only help if each adds distinct warrant; a fourth that proves nothing the first three did not dilutes the argument and signals you cannot tell strong evidence from filler, which itself undercuts the judgment a portfolio is meant to demonstrate. The economy test is removal: if cutting an artifact would not weaken the case, cut it. Keep only the smallest set that, taken together, would convince a skeptic of the whole claim, and make sure each survivor carries a rationale tag naming a contribution the others do not make.\"></self-explain>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_portfolio_artifact_selection\",\"cat531_supplement_final_project_and_professional_vision\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe dominant misreading is treating grade as warrant. A grade certifies that an instructor was satisfied; it rarely maps onto the specific professional competency a portfolio claim asserts, and reviewers know this. A second misreading is hoarding: including everything strong so the portfolio looks abundant. Abundance without economy reads as an inability to judge evidence, which undercuts the very competency a portfolio is meant to demonstrate.\n\nA subtler error is excluding artifacts that document a refusal or a near-miss. A reflection in which you rejected an unfit tool can be the most persuasive evidence of professional judgment precisely because it shows the value winning against convenience.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nSuppose your claim is \"designs assessments that AI cannot trivially complete for the student.\" A pride-first selection would feature your most elaborate project. A warrant-first selection asks what would prove this specific competency. Strong candidates: a revised assignment prompt that requires students to critique an AI draft rather than produce one (shows you redesigned for the AI era), a side-by-side of the old AI-vulnerable prompt and the new one (shows the reasoning), and a student work sample where the AI-resistant task surfaced genuine thinking (shows it worked).\n\nNow apply coverage and economy. The three artifacts cover design intent, the reasoning behind it, and evidence of effect, which together establish the claim; a fourth artifact showing a generic rubric would add no new warrant and should be cut. Tag each: \"redesigned prompt, shifts the cognitive demand from production to evaluation,\" \"before/after, makes the design reasoning auditable,\" \"student sample, evidence the redesign elicited thinking AI could not supply.\" A reviewer can now follow the argument from claim to proof without taking your word for any link.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read a portfolio (yours or a peer's), cover the artifacts and read only the claims; then predict what evidence each claim would need. Uncover the artifacts and check whether the actual selections match your predicted evidence or merely sit near it. The gaps you find are exactly where the portfolio substitutes pride for warrant. By the end of CAT 531 06.03 you should be able to look at any artifact and immediately name which claim, if any, it actually warrants.\n\nA strong reader of a portfolio can answer four questions:\n\n1. What specific claim does each artifact warrant, in one sentence?\n2. Does the set cover the whole claim or only the easiest part of it?\n3. Which artifact could be removed without weakening the argument?\n4. Is any artifact included for pride or polish rather than warrant?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA selection is strong when every artifact carries a rationale tag a skeptic would accept, and when removing any one artifact would visibly weaken the case. Polish is the last layer; the load-bearing layers are warrant, coverage, and economy. If an artifact lacks a defensible tag, mark it and ask BigAL to compare it against a stronger candidate for the same claim rather than to \"improve the portfolio.\"\n\nA useful closing line would read: \"I included the rejected-tool reflection over my A-graded deck because the claim is about equity judgment, not content mastery, and the reflection is the only artifact where that judgment is actually visible in action.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same portfolio from a job application to a National Board certification review. The artifacts may stay, but the governing claims change: certification asks for evidence of impact on student learning over time, not a snapshot of competence. An artifact that warranted \"designs equitable tasks\" for a hiring panel may now need a companion piece showing the task's effect across a unit. If you understood selection as matching evidence to a claim, you re-tag and re-cover for the new claims without panic. If you only assembled a fixed set of favorites, you will submit evidence that proves the wrong proposition for the new audience.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/06-03-portfolio-artifact-selection-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"06.03\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **TPACK**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 06.03. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_portfolio_artifact_selection\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace records evidence source, risk, support choice, and revision quality. That is what separates professional judgment from simple completion.\n\n## References\n\n- TPACK. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- AI and education datafication. https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_3_portfolio_artifact_selection_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_portfolio_artifact_selection","stem":"A candidate must select 3 artifacts for a professional portfolio that claims the competency \"uses technology to support equitable learning.\" Which selection best evidences that specific claim?","options":["The three assignments that received the highest grades, regardless of topic.","A lesson plan with a UDL access plan, a sample of differentiated AI-feedback settings, and a reflection on a tool she rejected for excluding students without devices.","Three polished slide decks that look the most professional.","The three most recent assignments, since they show current skill."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Artifacts are selected to evidence a named competency claim, not for grade, polish, or recency.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_6_3_portfolio_artifact_selection_grade_proxy","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_final_project_and_professional_vision","cat531_supplement_portfolio_artifact_selection","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_3_portfolio_artifact_selection_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_portfolio_artifact_selection","stem":"A reviewer annotates a portfolio: \"You included this artifact but never said what it proves. I cannot tell which standard it is evidence for.\" What is the missing element?","options":["A higher-resolution image of the artifact.","A rationale tag connecting the artifact to the specific competency or standard it evidences and how.","More artifacts so the section looks complete.","A statement that the artifact earned a good grade."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Each portfolio artifact needs an explicit rationale linking it to the claim it supports; selection without a rationale is just a file.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_6_3_portfolio_artifact_selection_no_rationale","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_portfolio_artifact_selection","evidence_based_revision"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_3_portfolio_artifact_selection_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"A candidate asks BigAL to recommend which artifacts to include. What is the defensible way to use that recommendation?","options":["Include exactly the set the model ranks highest, since it compared them objectively.","Use the model to cluster artifacts by which competency each could evidence, then the candidate decides the final set against the actual rubric and removes a strong-looking artifact that duplicates a claim.","Ask the model to write the rationale tags so they sound consistent.","Let the model decide because it removes personal bias from selection."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"AI can organize candidate evidence, but the curatorial judgment about what each artifact proves stays with the teacher.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_6_3_portfolio_artifact_selection_ai_curates","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_3_portfolio_artifact_selection_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_portfolio_artifact_selection","stem":"A portfolio includes one artifact that documents a lesson that went poorly, with the candidate's analysis of what she changed. A peer says to cut it because it shows failure. What is the strongest reason to keep it?","options":["Failure artifacts are required by the rubric.","It pads the portfolio to the expected length.","It evidences reflective judgment and growth, which is itself a target competency, as long as the rationale names what the revision shows.","Reviewers will not read it closely anyway."],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"An artifact's value is what claim it can defensibly evidence; a documented improvement can be strong evidence of professional judgment.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_6_3_portfolio_artifact_selection_only_successes_count","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_portfolio_artifact_selection","evidence_based_revision"]}]}}